{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Base directory for this skill: /home/administrator/.claude/skills/claude-shared/skills/refocus\n\n# /refocus — concept-bound session forking\n\n## Genre — read this first\n\nThis is a **session-routing skill**, not a content-generation skill. The work is mostly file IO + carefully curated narrative. Mechanical bits (UUID generation, INDEX regeneration, archival) live in `lib/`. The cognitive work — drafting a brief that's load-bearing without being a transcript dump, enumerating material_changes honestly — is yours.\n\nThe architectural source of truth is `DESIGN.md` (same dir). When in doubt about *why* a thing is the way it is, read DESIGN.md before improvising.\n\n---\n\n## Two commands. That's it.\n\n| Command | Side | Job |\n|---|---|---|\n| `/refocus <dest-dir> [--slug s] [--reason r] [--tier t] [--manual\\|--oneshot]` | parent | Draft brief, reserve `child_session_id`, write `<dest>/docs/refocus/<id>.md`, then **auto-spawn the child worker** (default) — or, with `--manual`/`--oneshot`, print a relaunch command for the human to run |\n| `/refocus-complete` | child (destination) | Append Result to brief, flip status, **chain `/context-save`** to promote material_changes |\n\n**Default is auto-spawn (execute).** `/refocus <dest>` with no mode flag launches the child itself via `lib/spawn-worker.sh` — the parent is re-invoked when the worker finishes and surfaces its Result. The old print-a-command behavior is opt-in: `--manual` (interactive) or `--oneshot` (headless). Rationale + history in DESIGN.md D2 / §7.\n\nAuto-discovery on session start is handled by `hooks/session-start.sh` — no command needed.\n\n---\n\n## /refocus — parent side\n\n### 0. Hard guard (D4)\n\nBefore anything else, check whether this session is itself a refocus child.\nInspect the SessionStart-hook stderr surfaced at session open: if it contained `🔀 refocus child:` or if the env shows `SESSION_REFOCUS_ROLE=child`, **refuse**. Tell the user:\n\n> This session is the child of refocus `<id>` at `<dest>`. Children are leaves — only parents can fork. Surface the work to spawn elsewhere in `Result.suggested_follow_ups` when you run `/refocus-complete`; the parent will decide whether to spawn a sibling.\n\nStop here. Do not proceed.\n\n### 1. Resolve the destination\n\nArgument: `<dest-dir>` — required, must already exist.\n\n- Resolve to absolute path (`realpath`).\n- Verify the dir exists. If not, refuse with: *\"Destination `<dest>` doesn't exist. Refocus is for existing project dirs; create the project first.\"* Do NOT auto-create.\n- Reject if `<dest>` == cwd. (Would be the same conceptual scope.)\n\n**Mode: the default is `execute` (auto-spawn).** With no mode flag the parent launches the headless worker itself via `lib/spawn-worker.sh` (see step 8). Two opt-in flags fall back to the old print-a-command behavior:\n\n- `--manual` — print an **interactive** `claude --session-id … \"<prompt>\"` relaunch command for the human to run (`spawn_mode=manual`). This was the pre-2026-07-18 default; it is now opt-in.\n- `--oneshot` — print a **headless** `claude -p` relaunch command for the human to run (`spawn_mode=oneshot`).\n- `--execute` — explicit form of the default (`spawn_mode=execute`); accepted as a no-op alias since execute is already the default.\n\n`--manual` and `--oneshot` are mutually exclusive. Both mean \"you (the parent) do NOT launch — you print a command the human copies.\"\n\nOptional flags:\n- `--slug <s>` — short-kebab-case slug for the work. If absent, ask the user for one (don't infer silently from filenames).\n- `--reason <r>` — one-line trigger; ends up in \"Why this branch exists\" alongside your richer paragraph.\n- `--tier <low|medium|high>` — difficulty dial for the (default) `execute` worker. One dial, three modes that escalate model first, then effort: **`low` = sonnet/high (default floor)**, **`medium` = opus/high**, **`high` = opus/max**. Effort floor is `high`, model floor is sonnet — neither goes lower (haiku and sub-high effort are refused). When the flag is absent, the parent auto-selects a tier per the rubric in step 8. (Advanced: `--model`/`--effort` can override either half of the pair, e.g. for a non-tier `sonnet/max`; normally use `--tier`.)\n\n**The execute worker's posture is deliberate:** it runs with `--dangerously-skip-permissions` and self-completes via `/refocus-complete`, so the brief's `done_when`/`out_of_scope` (plus the leaf guard D4 and the destructive-command hook) are the worker's only guardrails. Write them tightly. Use `--manual`/`--oneshot` when you want a human in the loop mid-run instead.\n\n### 2. Apply the drift rubric (DESIGN.md §6)\n\nTwo firing conditions, **both required**:\n\n1. **Subtree mismatch** — work would create/modify files in a different `~/projects/<X>/` subtree than current cwd.\n2. **Independent resumption test** — *\"If work stopped now, would the next session naturally start in `dest` rather than `src`?\"*\n\nIf either is no, propose staying in current session. Tell the user explicitly: *\"This doesn't feel like a refocus — finishing here, because <reason>. Override?\"*\n\n### 3. Draft the brief\n\nRead DESIGN.md §5 (\"Brief schema\"). The brief is **what the child needs to make good decisions about destination work**, written from your full source-session context, capped to what's relevant.\n\n- **Why this branch exists**: 1 short paragraph. The trigger.\n- **Inherited context**: 5–15 bullets. Load-bearing facts/decisions from this session that affect destination work. **Bullets, not prose. Each bullet a reusable fact.** Cap at ~300 words. If you exceed that, you're transcript-dumping — cut.\n- **Open questions / desired deliverables**: bullet list of what the child must produce.\n- **done_when** (frontmatter, ≥1 entry): success criteria the child uses as Definition of Done.\n- **out_of_scope** (frontmatter, ≥1 entry): explicit \"the child must NOT do X\" guards.\n\nShow the draft to the user; they confirm/edit each section before you write the file. Don't write silently — the brief curation is the point of `/refocus`. **This confirmation stays mandatory even under the execute default:** the worker runs autonomously with skip-permissions, so the brief's `done_when`/`out_of_scope` are its only rails — a bad brief means a bad autonomous run. Get the quick confirm *before* you launch (step 8). (`--manual`/`--oneshot` don't change this — the human still reviews the brief, they just launch it themselves afterward.)\n\n### 4. Generate the id pair\n\nRun the helper:\n\n```bash\n~/.claude/skills/claude-shared/skills/refocus/lib/id-gen.sh <slug>\n```\n\nIt prints two lines: `<id>` then `<child_session_id>` (UUIDv4). Capture both.\n\n### 5. Render the brief and write it\n\nRead `~/.claude/skills/claude-shared/skills/refocus/lib/brief-template.md` and substitute the placeholders (`__ID__`, `__CHILD_SESSION_ID__`, `__SOURCE_DIR__`, `__SOURCE_SESSION_ID__` from `$CLAUDE_SESSION_ID`, `__DEST_DIR__`, `__SLUG__`, `__SPAWN_MODE__` (`execute` — the default — unless `--manual` or `--oneshot` was passed), `__TIER__` (`low`|`medium`|`high` for `execute` per the step-8 rubric; `null` for `manual`/`oneshot` where the human picks), `__SPAWNED_AT__` (ISO 8601 UTC), `__TITLE__`, `__WHY__`, `__INHERITED_CONTEXT__`, `__DELIVERABLES__`, `__DONE_WHEN_1__` plus extra `done_when` items, `__OUT_OF_SCOPE_1__` plus extras, `__ENCODED_SOURCE_CWD__` (cwd with `/` → `-`)).\n\nWrite the result to `<dest>/docs/refocus/<id>.md`. Create `<dest>/docs/refocus/` if it doesn't exist.\n\n### 6. Project bookkeeping (one-time per project)\n\nFor both `<src>` and `<dest>`, idempotently:\n\n- **`.gitignore`** — append `docs/refocus/INDEX.md` if not already present (skill creates the file if missing).\n- **`CLAUDE.md`** — if the file exists and doesn't already reference `docs/refocus/INDEX.md`, **prepend** (right after the title `# ...` line):\n\n  ```markdown\n  > 🔀 **Session history (refocus)**: See [docs/refocus/INDEX.md](docs/refocus/INDEX.md) for incoming briefs and outbound spawns.\n  ```\n\n### 7. Regenerate INDEX.md (both sides)\n\n```bash\n~/.claude/skills/claude-shared/skills/refocus/lib/index-regen.sh <src>\n~/.claude/skills/claude-shared/skills/refocus/lib/index-regen.sh <dest>\n```\n\n### 8. Launch the worker (default), and drop the spawn marker\n\n**Default (execute) — you (the parent) launch the worker yourself. Don't print anything.** Run the helper, which skip-marks, stamps the brief, builds the prompt (brief + autonomous-worker directive), and runs `claude -p` to completion. This is the default path — no mode flag needed. Skip to \"Pick the tier first\" below.\n\n**Only with `--manual` or `--oneshot` — print the relaunch command** literally so the user copies it (you do NOT launch):\n\nThe `--manual` interactive command MUST carry an initial prompt that tells the child to read and\nexecute the brief. Launching with `--session-id` alone opens a session that just waits for\ninput (the SessionStart hook surfaces the brief into context, but nothing makes Claude take\na turn) — the common \"I ran it and it did nothing.\" Keep `--session-id` (it's what lets the\nchild's `/refocus-complete` locate its brief by matching `$CLAUDE_SESSION_ID`); add the\nprompt so the child self-starts. (The execute default avoids this footgun entirely — `spawn-worker.sh` always feeds the brief via stdin.)\n\n```\nBrief written: <dest>/docs/refocus/<id>.md\n\nWith --manual, launch the child session:\n  cd <dest> && claude --session-id <child_session_id> \"Read docs/refocus/<id>.md and carry out the brief end to end. Run /refocus-complete when done.\"\n\nWith --oneshot, instead:\n  cd <dest> && claude --session-id <child_session_id> -p \"$(cat <dest>/docs/refocus/<id>.md)\"\n```\n\n**For `--oneshot` (autonomous worker, user-launched):** mark the session so it doesn't become `claudeauto`'s auto-resume target in `<dest>` (else the next `claudeauto <dest>` lands in the dead worker, not the user's real session):\n\n```bash\n~/.claude/skills/claude-shared/skills/refocus/lib/skip-mark.sh <child_session_id>\n```\n\nThis appends `<child_session_id>` to `~/.claude/claudeauto-skip`. The transcript filename is untouched, so the worker stays manually resumable via `claude --resume <child_session_id>` and still shows in the `--resume` picker — only `claudeauto`'s auto-pick skips it. **Do NOT run this for `--manual` interactive children:** those are real hands-on sessions the user may legitimately want `claudeauto` to auto-resume. (The execute default handles its own skip-marking inside `spawn-worker.sh` — see below.)\n\n**Pick the tier first** (execute default). You are the classifier — you already have the context from drafting the brief. The dial has three modes (escalate model before effort); default to **`low`** and step up deliberately:\n\n| Tier | = model / effort | Use when |\n|---|---|---|\n| **low** *(default)* | sonnet / high | Bounded, well-specified work; read-only or easily-reversible; verify-and-plan, mechanical edits, doc generation. |\n| **medium** | opus / high | Needs stronger judgment — real design/architecture decisions, security reasoning, ambiguous requirements, multi-file changes — **or** mutates real-but-recoverable state. |\n| **high** | opus / max | The hardest **and/or** highest blast-radius — novel architecture, subtle security-critical logic, unknown-root-cause debugging, or irreversible/live-infra mutation. |\n\nWhen genuinely torn between two tiers, pick the lower (the operator's posture is \"escalate as a deliberate lever, not by default\"). If the user passed `--tier` (or `--model`/`--effort`), honor it verbatim. Record the chosen tier in the brief frontmatter `tier:` field.\n\n```bash\n~/.claude/skills/claude-shared/skills/refocus/lib/spawn-worker.sh <dest_dir> <child_session_id> <dest_dir>/docs/refocus/<id>.md [--tier medium|high]\n```\n\nRun it under **`Bash run_in_background=true`** so you (the parent) are re-invoked when the worker exits rather than blocking on it. The helper already calls `skip-mark.sh` — do **not** also run skip-mark for `--execute`. When the background job completes:\n\n1. Read `<dest>/docs/refocus/<id>.md` — the worker's `/refocus-complete` will have appended the **Result** block (status, summary, artifacts, follow-ups). Read `<id>.worker.log` next to it only if you need the worker's raw reasoning.\n2. Surface the Result to the user: status, one-line summary, any `suggested_follow_ups` (which you, the parent, decide whether to spawn as siblings).\n3. If the helper reported a timeout (rc=124) or the Result is missing, tell the user the worker didn't self-complete and point at the log.\n\nIn your text reply, also drop a one-line marker so cross-session search finds the fork point: *\"SPAWNED `<id>` → `<dest_dir>` (child_session_id: `<UUID>`, mode: `<manual|oneshot|execute>`, tier: `<low|medium|high>` = `<model>/<effort>` — `<one-line reason for the tier choice>`). Awaiting result.\"*\n\n---\n\n## /refocus --later — finding routing (D6, the cold speed)\n\nSame primitive as `/refocus`, colder handler. Where the default (`--now`) spawns a session to do the\nwork now, `--later` deposits a **finding** into a *different owner's* co-located context — a durable\ninbound item they triage on **their** clock. Use it when you (in project A) discover a defect owned by\nproject B and B's owner is the right actor to fix it (they have B's co-located knowledge; you don't, and\nthe 1-hop rule bars you from fixing it in A's session).\n\n**Handler is a sovereign, not a subordinate.** Unlike a `--now` child (which you orchestrate and which\ncan only return `blocked`), B's owner may accept / triage / **reject** / dedup / fix on their own clock.\nThat is why closure is owned by the **GitLab issue**, not a `/refocus-complete` Result. The `<id>.md` at\nthe dest is a birth-certificate mirror; a reconcile step syncs its `status` and archives it on issue close.\n\n**Invocation:** `/refocus --later <dest-dir> --slug <s> --severity <blocker|high|medium|low>`\n\nSteps (all mechanics live in `lib/later.sh`, loaded only on this path — `--now` stays lean):\n\n1. **Guard:** the leaf guard (D4) is **exempt** for `--later` — a fire-and-forget finding spawns no\n   session/tree/loop, so a child/`--execute` worker in A *may* route a finding to B. (D4 still blocks a\n   child from `--now`/spawn.)\n2. **Curate the finding brief.** Write the repro / root-cause / evidence / suggested-fix to a body file\n   (this is the \"Inherited context\"). No `done_when`/`out_of_scope`/`--tier` (nothing is spawned).\n3. **Dispatch:**\n   ```bash\n   ~/.claude/skills/claude-shared/skills/refocus/lib/later.sh \\\n     --dest <dest-dir> --slug <slug> --severity <sev> \\\n     --title \"<one-line title>\" --body-file <body.md> \\\n     --source-dir \"$PWD\" --source-session \"$CLAUDE_SESSION_ID\" [--notify]\n   ```\n   It: resolves `dest → GitLab project` from the dest's git remote (fails loudly if none/non-GitLab);\n   ensures the `enhancement::inbox` + `dedup::<key>` labels exist; **dedups** against open issues carrying\n   the same `dedup::<key>` (match → appends the new evidence as a comment, does **not** silently drop);\n   else files a card into B's **inbox** (`enhancement::inbox` + `backlog` + `type::bug` + `severity::`)\n   carrying the brief and a back-link to the source session. It warns if B has no `inbox` *board* — the\n   label is the lane, the board is only the human's view of it.\n   **No file is written anywhere** — the card is the sole record (D6).\n   `--notify` (high/blocker only) best-effort pings `#cicd-notifications` if a notifier is wired.\n   `--dry-run` previews and touches nothing — no API call, no file.\n   All GitLab traffic goes through `glab api`; there is no raw `curl` and no second auth path.\n4. **Report** the one-line marker: *\"FILED finding `<id>` → `<dest>` (issue `<url>`, severity `<sev>`,\n   dedup `<key>`).\"*\n\n### Dedup is keyed on the slug, not the title\n\n`dedup_key = sha256(target_project + \"\\n\" + slug)[:12]`, carried as a `dedup::<key>` label and as an\n`<!-- dedup::<key> -->` marker in the description.\n\n**Not the title.** Titles are mutable — a triager rewords the card, and a title-derived key can no longer\nbe recomputed, so the next sighting files a duplicate. That is the failure `--later` exists to prevent,\nand it was real: hashing `administrators/mcp!2`'s own current title yielded a key different from the\nlabel it carried. The slug is the finding's identity: the caller chooses it, `id-gen.sh` constrains it to\n`^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,63}$`, and it never appears as editable card text.\n\nConsequence, accepted on purpose: **two distinct findings filed to the same project under the same slug\nare treated as one sighting.** Give a genuinely new finding a new slug.\n\n### Tests\n\n`lib/tests/run.sh` — 67 cases across `authz`, `scrub`, `later`. Pure: no network, no credential, no card\n(argument errors and `--dry-run` both return before secrets load). Run them before touching `later.sh`.\n\n### The brief is a card artifact\n\nThe brief rides **on the card**, fenced between HTML-comment markers that render invisible:\n\n```\n<!-- refocus:brief:v1:begin -->\n…the curated brief, byte-for-byte…\n<!-- refocus:brief:v1:end -->\n```\n\nA worker picking the card up recovers it exactly:\n\n```bash\n~/.claude/skills/claude-shared/skills/refocus/lib/brief-extract.sh <group/project> <iid> > brief.md\n```\n\nRound-trip is byte-identical, up to a single trailing newline appended when the brief lacks one.\n`later.sh` **refuses to file** a brief containing the end marker rather than emit a corrupt artifact.\n\nDeliberately *not* a GitLab upload attachment: an attachment is a second store that drifts from the\ncard the moment someone edits the description, and its `/uploads/<hash>/` URL outlives the issue it\nhung off — the exact failure mode D6 removed by deleting the local file. (`glab api` also cannot do\nmultipart, so an upload would have meant reintroducing `curl` and a second auth path.)\n\n**Prereq (owner-side opt-in):** B must have granted the source Reporter — \"route to co-located context\"\nis only coherent when B consented to receive. `later.sh` creates `enhancement::inbox` idempotently;\naccess, and the `inbox` board itself (`/gitlab-board setup`), are the operator's responsibility.\n\nThe card lands in `backlog`, which is **inert**: AI never promotes `backlog` → `ready`. Filing into\nanother project's inbox cannot make its agent act; a human must move the card first.\n\n### Dispatch policy, and where the boundary actually is\n\n`later.sh` refuses (exit 5) before it files anything when either holds:\n\n| Rule | Refuses |\n|---|---|\n| **The one trust rule** | `dev → admin`. `admin→dev`, `dev→dev`, `admin→admin` are allowed — developer-to-developer risk is accepted; the asymmetric case is a developer reaching an administrator-tier worker. |\n| **Reporter gate** | caller has `< 20` (Reporter) on the target project. |\n\nCaller tier is read from the **GitLab identity the token resolves to** (`GET /user` + `administrators`\ngroup membership), never from `id -nG` — the linux group is a proxy that can disagree with whichever\ntoken was sourced. The caller's `GL_TOKEN`/`GL_HOST` win over `~/projects/secrets/gitlab.env`; the file\nonly fills gaps, so a developer or CI job authenticates as itself.\n\nThe decision is a pure function in `lib/authz.sh`, so every tier combination is tested without minting\na credential (`lib/tests/authz_test.sh`). `later.sh --check-authz` evaluates the live path and prints\nthe verdict without filing.\n\n**None of this is a trust boundary.** `later.sh` runs as the caller with the caller's token — anyone\nwho wants around it simply doesn't run it. It buys a legible refusal instead of an opaque 403, and an\nhonest audit line in the source transcript. The boundaries that hold are:\n\n1. **GitLab membership**, enforced server-side. A developer with no Reporter on `administrators/*`\n   cannot create the issue regardless of what this script decides. Keep that grant off `developers`.\n2. **The consumer.** A filed card is *inert*. The risk begins where something picks an `inbox` card up\n   and spawns a session from it — i.e. `--live` and the listener, not `--later`. That is where\n   `dev → admin` must be enforced for real.\n\n---\n\n## /refocus-complete — destination side\n\nRun this when work at the destination is done (success **or** blocker).\n\n### 1. Locate this session's brief\n\nThe brief is at `<cwd>/docs/refocus/<id>.md` where `<id>`'s `child_session_id` matches `$CLAUDE_SESSION_ID`. The `hooks/session-start.sh` already surfaced it on session open. If multiple briefs claim this session id (shouldn't happen but), refuse and ask the user which.\n\n### 2. Gather the Result content (mandatory enumeration)\n\n**First check `spawn_mode` in the brief frontmatter:**\n- **`oneshot` or `execute` (headless worker)** — there is NO interactive user to ask or confirm with. Fill ALL sections below from your own session context and proceed straight through steps 3–6 without prompting. This is the autonomous completion path (the `--execute` worker depends on it). Material changes still mandatory — enumerate from what you actually changed, or write `N/A — investigation only`.\n- **`manual` (interactive child)** — proceed as below: ask the user, or fill from your own session context then confirm with them.\n\nFor each section (**Material changes is mandatory** — you cannot skip it; pure-investigation refocuses must explicitly write `N/A — investigation only`; this is the cognitive layer of D5):\n\n- **Status** — `completed` or `blocked`. (`blocked` means a follow-up requiring work elsewhere is needed; parent will orchestrate.)\n- **Definition-of-Done met** — checklist mirroring `done_when` from frontmatter; each item ✓ or note why not.\n- **Summary** — 1 paragraph: what was accomplished or where it blocked.\n- **Artifacts produced** — bullet list: `<path> — <one-line description>`.\n- **Suggested follow-ups (parent decides)** — bullet list: items the child noticed that belong elsewhere. Each entry: `<dir>` + `<slug>` + one-line reason.\n- **Material changes (for /context-save)** — list which `docs/context/*.md` files at `<dest>` should be updated and the one-line summary, OR explicit `N/A — investigation only, no canonical state changed.`\n\n### 3. Append to the brief\n\nInsert the Result block after the `## Result\\n<empty until child writes>` placeholder in `<id>.md` (replace the placeholder line). Keep the YAML frontmatter intact.\n\nUpdate the frontmatter:\n- `status: result` if Status=completed; `status: blocked` if Status=blocked.\n- `completed_at:` ISO 8601 UTC.\n\n### 4. Mechanical layer of D5 — chain /context-save\n\nInvoke `/context-save` for the destination. Surface its output. **Do not let context-save errors block the status flip** — log + report any failure clearly, but the brief is already at status=result. The user can fix and rerun `/context-save` separately.\n\n### 5. Regenerate INDEX.md\n\n```bash\n~/.claude/skills/claude-shared/skills/refocus/lib/index-regen.sh <dest>\n```\n\n(The source side will reconcile on its own next resume via `hooks/session-start.sh`.)\n\n### 6. Print the close\n\nTell the user:\n- The brief is at `<id>.md` with status=`<result|blocked>`.\n- The full source-session jsonl pointer for cross-reference.\n- \"Source session will see this on its next resume.\"\n\n---\n\n## Edge cases (from DESIGN.md §9 — quick recall)\n\n- **Brief is too thin** — child can `Read` source jsonl pointer to expand. Self-correcting.\n- **Brief is too thick** — schema cap (~300w on Inherited context) applies; warn user if exceeded.\n- **Wrong destination** — edit `<id>.md` frontmatter to `status: abandoned`, add `reason:`. Re-run `/refocus` for correct dest.\n- **Child hits a blocker** — `Status: blocked` + `suggested_follow_ups`. Parent fans out clearing children, then re-spawns blocked work as fresh refocus with `parent_refocus_id` chained.\n- **User never launches** — source's next `/refocus` checks for own outgoing briefs >24h at status=reserved, prompts to abandon.\n- **Launched but did nothing (empty Result / dead session)** — a **`--manual`/`--oneshot`** child opened but never worked the brief (almost always: hand-launched with `--session-id` but no initial prompt, so it just waited; or it got auto-flipped to `completed` with an empty `## Result`). The execute default does not hit this — `spawn-worker.sh` always pipes the brief in — so if it recurs on the default path, read `<id>.worker.log` for the real failure (timeout rc=124, crash, or refusal). To re-run a manual relaunch: reset the brief frontmatter (`status: reserved`, `launched_at: null`, `completed_at: null`), **mint a fresh `child_session_id`** (the consumed id can collide on relaunch — `cat /proc/sys/kernel/random/uuid`, replace every occurrence in `<id>.md`), regen the dest INDEX, then hand over the initial-prompt launch command from step 8. Never reuse a consumed session-id for a relaunch.\n- **Refocus chain >5 deep** — warn (likely design smell); don't block.\n\n---\n\n## What this skill is NOT (DESIGN.md §10)\n\n- Not `/compact` (which trims this session). `/refocus` redirects.\n- Not agent-memory (which stores cross-project bullet-facts). Briefs are project-scoped, transcript-shaped reasoning. Test for the rare exception: see DESIGN.md §8d.\n- Not subagents (transcripts under parent's session dir, breaks the dest-discoverability property).\n- Not `/init` (assumes project exists).\n- Not project management — briefs are not tickets.\n\n---\n\n## Reference\n\n- **DESIGN.md** — same dir. The architectural source of truth. Read first when uncertain.\n- **Public deck** — https://nginx.ai-servicers.com/presentations/refocus/\n- **Downloadable design** — https://nginx.ai-servicers.com/presentations/refocus/refocus-design-v2.1.md\n\n\nARGUMENTS: complete"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"59a355ba-da00-4059-aa80-2a345be94b30","uuid":"4a5c9ff7-4191-4141-8f61-629e5aabc751","timestamp":"2026-07-28T22:22:28.517Z","isSynthetic":true}
