{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DZoRwgo8eMSaV2t3tiETJ3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tid: 2026-07-28-mail-cert-san-59a355\n3\tstatus: in-progress\n4\tchild_session_id: 59a355ba-da00-4059-aa80-2a345be94b30\n5\tspawn_mode: execute\n6\ttier: low\n7\tspawned_at: 2026-07-28T15:20:00Z\n8\tlaunched_at: 2026-07-28T22:16:27Z\n9\tcompleted_at: null\n10\tsource_dir: /home/administrator/projects/aws\n11\tsource_session_id: 700184f3-55a6-4052-b9fa-225f8550bc2e\n12\tdest_dir: /home/administrator/projects/docker-mailserver\n13\tslug: mail-cert-san\n14\tparent_refocus_id: 2026-07-28-proxy-protocol-edge-ca1077\n15\trelated_refocus_ids: []\n16\tdone_when:\n17\t  - \"docker exec mailserver openssl x509 -in /etc/dms/tls/cert -noout -ext subjectAltName includes mail.ai-servicers.com (via the *.ai-servicers.com wildcard).\"\n18\t  - \"A TLS connection to mail.ai-servicers.com:993 validates with peer verification ON — the exact condition Nextcloud Mail requires.\"\n19\t  - \"mailserver and mail-edge-wg are both healthy afterwards, and port 25 through the AWS edge (34.194.247.228) still answers 220.\"\n20\t  - \"The bind-mount change and its reason are recorded in CLAUDE.md.\"\n21\tout_of_scope:\n22\t  - \"Do NOT modify Traefik, traefik-certs-dumper, or acme.json. The extraction is CORRECT — it already writes both certificates. Nothing there is broken.\"\n23\t  - \"Do NOT write to the traefik-certs directory under the platform data tree. It is hard-denied by the destructive-guard hook. Reference it from the compose file only; inspect it read-only via `docker exec` into a container that already mounts it.\"\n24\t  - \"Do NOT disable TLS peer verification anywhere, in Nextcloud or elsewhere. That converts the symptom into a silent security hole.\"\n25\t  - \"Do NOT change MX records, any DNS, or anything in ~/projects/aws.\"\n26\t  - \"Do NOT touch Nextcloud. Deleting or repointing Mail account id 3 belongs to the projects/nextcloud session.\"\n27\trelated: []\n28\t---\n29\t\n30\t# Brief: serve the wildcard cert so mail.ai-servicers.com validates\n31\t\n32\t## Why this branch exists\n33\tNextcloud Mail cannot open IMAP: `mailserver` presents a certificate whose only SAN is\n34\t`ai-servicers.com`, so validating clients reject `mail.ai-servicers.com`. The\n35\t`projects/nextcloud` session diagnosed the symptom correctly but concluded Traefik's\n36\tcertificate extraction was faulty. It is not. The dumper writes **both** certificates into\n37\tseparate directories; `docker-mailserver` simply bind-mounts the wrong one. The fix is one\n38\tline here, and Traefik needs no change at all.\n39\t\n40\t## Inherited context\n41\t- `traefik-certs-dumper` runs `file --version v3 --domain-subdir --source /traefik/acme.json\n42\t  --dest /certs --watch`, producing one directory per certificate.\n43\t- Verified contents of that output directory (read-only, via `docker exec` into the dumper):\n44\t  - `*.ai-servicers.com/` → `certificate.crt`, `privatekey.key`  ← **the wildcard, what we want**\n45\t  - `ai-servicers.com/`   → `certificate.crt`, `privatekey.key`  ← apex-only, what we serve today\n46\t  - plus `diagrams.nginx.…`, `registry.gitlab.…`, and `private/letsencrypt.key`\n47\t- `acme.json` holds 4 certificates including **separate** `ai-servicers.com` and\n48\t  `*.ai-servicers.com` entries. Both are legitimately issued; this is not a mis-issuance.\n49\t- `mailserver` is `SSL_TYPE=manual`, `SSL_CERT_PATH=/certs/certificate.crt`,\n50\t  `SSL_KEY_PATH=/certs/privatekey.key`. Its certificate bind mount currently targets the\n51\t  **`ai-servicers.com`** subdirectory.\n52\t- Confirmed served SAN today: `DNS:ai-servicers.com` only. Confirmed wildcard on Traefik's\n53\t  `:443`: `DNS:*.ai-servicers.com, DNS:ai-servicers.com`.\n54\t- **Gotcha:** the target directory is literally named `*.ai-servicers.com`. A glob character\n55\t  in a path. Quote it correctly in YAML and in any shell command, or it will silently expand\n56\t  or fail to match.\n57\t- **Gotcha:** restarting `mail-edge-wg` kills `mailserver` with exit 128 — `mailserver` uses\n58\t  `network_mode: service:mail-edge-wg` and loses the namespace. Recovery is\n59\t  `docker compose up -d mailserver`. Restarting `mailserver` alone is safe and is all this\n60\t  change needs.\n61\t- **MX now points at this server.** Mail is live and no longer forwards to Gmail, so downtime\n62\t  during this change is real lost mail, not a lab inconvenience. Keep the restart tight.\n63\t- Nextcloud reaches IMAP only through the public `34.194.247.228:993` — its container resolver\n64\t  returns the AWS Elastic IP, not the LAN address. The certificate must therefore validate on\n65\t  the public path; a LAN-only fix does not help it.\n66\t- Ports 25/465/587/993 all mandate PROXY protocol (`haproxy_trusted_networks = 10.77.0.1/32`).\n67\t  Port 143 is plain and published LAN-only on `192.168.1.13`. Use 143 for local probes; use the\n68\t  edge for the validating probe.\n69\t\n70\t## Open questions / desired deliverables\n71\t- Point the certificate bind mount at the wildcard directory and restart `mailserver`.\n72\t- Prove the served certificate now covers `mail.ai-servicers.com`.\n73\t- Prove a peer-verifying TLS client succeeds against `mail.ai-servicers.com:993` — that is the\n74\t  precise condition Nextcloud Mail needs, and the reason this work exists.\n75\t- Decide whether to reference the wildcard directory directly or via a stable symlink or an\n76\t  `SSL_CERT_PATH` change; pick one and record why. A directory containing a glob character is a\n77\t  foot-gun for whoever edits this next.\n78\t- Record the change and both gotchas above in `CLAUDE.md`.\n79\t\n80\t## Hard rule for child\n81\t- Children are leaves. If you discover work that belongs in a different\n82\t  directory, do NOT call /refocus. Surface it in Result.suggested_follow_ups\n83\t  for the parent to decide.\n84\t\n85\t## Pointer back\n86\t- Source session: `~/.claude/projects/-home-administrator-projects-aws/700184f3-55a6-4052-b9fa-225f8550bc2e.jsonl`\n87\t- Originating issue brief: `~/projects/aws/nextcloud.issue.md`, reply in `~/projects/aws/nextcloud.response.md`.\n88\t- To resume this child's session: `cd /home/administrator/projects/docker-mailserver && claude --resume 59a355ba-da00-4059-aa80-2a345be94b30`\n89\t\n90\t---\n91\t\n92\t## Result\n93\t<empty until child writes>\n94\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"59a355ba-da00-4059-aa80-2a345be94b30","uuid":"51b485fd-ac40-4481-a3a6-7ac0df24fe2b","timestamp":"2026-07-28T22:22:37.139Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/administrator/projects/docker-mailserver/docs/refocus/2026-07-28-mail-cert-san-59a355.md","content":"---\nid: 2026-07-28-mail-cert-san-59a355\nstatus: in-progress\nchild_session_id: 59a355ba-da00-4059-aa80-2a345be94b30\nspawn_mode: execute\ntier: low\nspawned_at: 2026-07-28T15:20:00Z\nlaunched_at: 2026-07-28T22:16:27Z\ncompleted_at: null\nsource_dir: /home/administrator/projects/aws\nsource_session_id: 700184f3-55a6-4052-b9fa-225f8550bc2e\ndest_dir: /home/administrator/projects/docker-mailserver\nslug: mail-cert-san\nparent_refocus_id: 2026-07-28-proxy-protocol-edge-ca1077\nrelated_refocus_ids: []\ndone_when:\n  - \"docker exec mailserver openssl x509 -in /etc/dms/tls/cert -noout -ext subjectAltName includes mail.ai-servicers.com (via the *.ai-servicers.com wildcard).\"\n  - \"A TLS connection to mail.ai-servicers.com:993 validates with peer verification ON — the exact condition Nextcloud Mail requires.\"\n  - \"mailserver and mail-edge-wg are both healthy afterwards, and port 25 through the AWS edge (34.194.247.228) still answers 220.\"\n  - \"The bind-mount change and its reason are recorded in CLAUDE.md.\"\nout_of_scope:\n  - \"Do NOT modify Traefik, traefik-certs-dumper, or acme.json. The extraction is CORRECT — it already writes both certificates. Nothing there is broken.\"\n  - \"Do NOT write to the traefik-certs directory under the platform data tree. It is hard-denied by the destructive-guard hook. Reference it from the compose file only; inspect it read-only via `docker exec` into a container that already mounts it.\"\n  - \"Do NOT disable TLS peer verification anywhere, in Nextcloud or elsewhere. That converts the symptom into a silent security hole.\"\n  - \"Do NOT change MX records, any DNS, or anything in ~/projects/aws.\"\n  - \"Do NOT touch Nextcloud. Deleting or repointing Mail account id 3 belongs to the projects/nextcloud session.\"\nrelated: []\n---\n\n# Brief: serve the wildcard cert so mail.ai-servicers.com validates\n\n## Why this branch exists\nNextcloud Mail cannot open IMAP: `mailserver` presents a certificate whose only SAN is\n`ai-servicers.com`, so validating clients reject `mail.ai-servicers.com`. The\n`projects/nextcloud` session diagnosed the symptom correctly but concluded Traefik's\ncertificate extraction was faulty. It is not. The dumper writes **both** certificates into\nseparate directories; `docker-mailserver` simply bind-mounts the wrong one. The fix is one\nline here, and Traefik needs no change at all.\n\n## Inherited context\n- `traefik-certs-dumper` runs `file --version v3 --domain-subdir --source /traefik/acme.json\n  --dest /certs --watch`, producing one directory per certificate.\n- Verified contents of that output directory (read-only, via `docker exec` into the dumper):\n  - `*.ai-servicers.com/` → `certificate.crt`, `privatekey.key`  ← **the wildcard, what we want**\n  - `ai-servicers.com/`   → `certificate.crt`, `privatekey.key`  ← apex-only, what we serve today\n  - plus `diagrams.nginx.…`, `registry.gitlab.…`, and `private/letsencrypt.key`\n- `acme.json` holds 4 certificates including **separate** `ai-servicers.com` and\n  `*.ai-servicers.com` entries. Both are legitimately issued; this is not a mis-issuance.\n- `mailserver` is `SSL_TYPE=manual`, `SSL_CERT_PATH=/certs/certificate.crt`,\n  `SSL_KEY_PATH=/certs/privatekey.key`. Its certificate bind mount currently targets the\n  **`ai-servicers.com`** subdirectory.\n- Confirmed served SAN today: `DNS:ai-servicers.com` only. Confirmed wildcard on Traefik's\n  `:443`: `DNS:*.ai-servicers.com, DNS:ai-servicers.com`.\n- **Gotcha:** the target directory is literally named `*.ai-servicers.com`. A glob character\n  in a path. Quote it correctly in YAML and in any shell command, or it will silently expand\n  or fail to match.\n- **Gotcha:** restarting `mail-edge-wg` kills `mailserver` with exit 128 — `mailserver` uses\n  `network_mode: service:mail-edge-wg` and loses the namespace. Recovery is\n  `docker compose up -d mailserver`. Restarting `mailserver` alone is safe and is all this\n  change needs.\n- **MX now points at this server.** Mail is live and no longer forwards to Gmail, so downtime\n  during this change is real lost mail, not a lab inconvenience. Keep the restart tight.\n- Nextcloud reaches IMAP only through the public `34.194.247.228:993` — its container resolver\n  returns the AWS Elastic IP, not the LAN address. The certificate must therefore validate on\n  the public path; a LAN-only fix does not help it.\n- Ports 25/465/587/993 all mandate PROXY protocol (`haproxy_trusted_networks = 10.77.0.1/32`).\n  Port 143 is plain and published LAN-only on `192.168.1.13`. Use 143 for local probes; use the\n  edge for the validating probe.\n\n## Open questions / desired deliverables\n- Point the certificate bind mount at the wildcard directory and restart `mailserver`.\n- Prove the served certificate now covers `mail.ai-servicers.com`.\n- Prove a peer-verifying TLS client succeeds against `mail.ai-servicers.com:993` — that is the\n  precise condition Nextcloud Mail needs, and the reason this work exists.\n- Decide whether to reference the wildcard directory directly or via a stable symlink or an\n  `SSL_CERT_PATH` change; pick one and record why. A directory containing a glob character is a\n  foot-gun for whoever edits this next.\n- Record the change and both gotchas above in `CLAUDE.md`.\n\n## Hard rule for child\n- Children are leaves. If you discover work that belongs in a different\n  directory, do NOT call /refocus. Surface it in Result.suggested_follow_ups\n  for the parent to decide.\n\n## Pointer back\n- Source session: `~/.claude/projects/-home-administrator-projects-aws/700184f3-55a6-4052-b9fa-225f8550bc2e.jsonl`\n- Originating issue brief: `~/projects/aws/nextcloud.issue.md`, reply in `~/projects/aws/nextcloud.response.md`.\n- To resume this child's session: `cd /home/administrator/projects/docker-mailserver && claude --resume 59a355ba-da00-4059-aa80-2a345be94b30`\n\n---\n\n## Result\n<empty until child writes>\n","numLines":94,"startLine":1,"totalLines":94}}}
